          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>In the case of a living donor transplant, the recipient can be in the&#x000a0;early stages&#x000a0;of end-stage renal disease (ESRD) and not require dialysis. Living donor kidney transplants have shown better graft survival and function.&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Pancreas Transplant&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>The causes of pancreatic failure are diabetes mellitus type 1 and 2, chronic pancreatitis, pancreatic cancer, biliary duct cancer, and graft failure. Pancreas transplant can be performed alone, simultaneously with a kidney transplant, or after a kidney transplant.&#x000a0; &#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Liver Transplant</bold>&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Table 2 </bold>describes the causes of liver failure requiring a transplant.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-132869.table1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 2. Causes of Liver Failure&#x000a0;</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px; height: 1px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
